m_{\\mathrm{aligned}\\,i,j} h_i(1-h_j) \\leq t \\cdot \\text{scale}\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nThe SDP relaxation augments variables via \n\\[\nz_{ij} = [\\begin{array}{c|c} h_i & \\sqrt{h_i(1-h_j)} \\\\ 1-h_i & \\sqrt{(1-h_i)(1-h_j)} \\end{array}]\n\\]\nfor each relevant $(i,j)$  \nand uses the fact that this structure preserves key integrality.\n\nThe objective value in the SDP is minimized by an interior-point method guaranteeing solution to a specific precision.\n\n### 3. Implementation procedure\n\nKey algorithms and helper functions must include:\n- **Initial Projection**: The provided project_feasible() function is used.\n- **Structure Generation**: A new mathematical function is created to generate the reformulation's blossoms from the grid structure.\n- **SDP Solution**: The CVXPY interface is used with the MOSEK solver (assuming availability).\n- **Rounders**: Four different rounding procedures are implemented:\n    1. Thresholding to mean\n    2. Gradient-based thresholding\n    3. Linial-Nisan type rounding\n    4. Interval minorization\n\nThe core implementation consists of:\n```python\nimport cvxpy as cp\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.special import entr\n\ndef compute_overlap(h):\n    # Original code: compute_c5(h)\n\ndef build_sdp_model(X, target, eigenbasis=None, weighting=1.0, low_rank=False):\n    \"\"\"\n    Constructs the SDP relaxation model which...\n    \"\"\"\n    # Replaces typical formation via cones and matrix comprehensions\n\ndef doit(X, target, seed=42):\n    # Builds SDP model\n    model = build_sdp_model(X, target)\n    # Sets solvers and solves\n```\n\n### 4. Search choices and useful variations\n\n- **Integration regimes**:\n    1. Small scale (n<=100): more aggressive rounding\n    2. Slightly larger (100<n<=400): hybrid approach\n    3. Full scale (n=400-2000): confidence-based minimal rounding\n\n- **Key Parameters**:\n    \u2022 Eigenvalue decomposition frequency\n    \u2022 Minimal rounding deviation\n    \u2022 Hybrid rounding crossover point\n\n- **Fallback**: If MOSEK unavailability is detected, falls back to iterative projection+coord_descent.\n\n### 5. Compute budget and robustness\n\nThe method will use:\n- CVXOPT or MOSEK: 300-700s per SDP instance\n- Rounding: 50-200s\n- Total: Around 600s for n=400\n\nEfficiency metrics:\n- 1300/600 \u2248 2-3 full iterations\n- Approx. 2.5 roundings per candidate\n- Properly maintained within limits\n\nRisk factors:\n- Overhead of SDP setup: 10%-20% of code time\n- Numerical instability for very large n\n\n### 6. Validation and return contract\n\nThe code returns:\n    (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n\nEach candidate is first projected via:\n```python\ndef ensure_feasibility(h, target_sum):\n    # Performs clipping and repair as needed\n```\n\nThe objective computation is:\n```python\ndef compute_c5(h):\n    # np.correlate implementation\n```\n\nMust enforce:\n    0<=h[i]<=1 for all i\n    |sum(h)*dx - 1| < 1e-6\n    c5_value is finite\n\nThis algorithm will be more competitive than local search approaches for benchmark instances, though marginally slower for the given n=400.",
